#pagebreak(weak: true)
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[QUESTIONS FOR REFLECTION])
#v(0.1in)

#set enum(spacing: 0.9em, indent: 0pt, body-indent: 0.6em)

+ Augustine is already convinced in his mind but cannot act (VIII.1.1,
  VIII.5.10). Where do you know what is right and still hold back?

+ "Give me chastity and continence, but not yet" (VIII.7.17). What is your
  "not yet"?

+ Three conversion stories (Victorinus, Antony, the two officials) move
  him more than arguments did. Whose story has moved you toward God?

+ He describes two wills fighting in one person (VIII.5.10-12, VIII.9.21).
  Is that your experience? How does Paul's "law in my members" help?

+ A child's chant, overheard by chance, sends him to the Bible
  (VIII.12.29). How do you understand moments like that?

+ The book ends not in private relief but in telling Alypius and Monica
  (VIII.12.30). Why does conversion need to be shared?
